        'Picture a Sunday lunch at a farmhouse in southern Estonia. The great-grandmother at the head of the table remembers empty shop shelves and a country that no longer exists. Her daughter remembers singing with thousands of people on the Song Festival Grounds in a summer of hope. Her grandson bought his first bicycle with kroons. His teenage daughter has never held a kroon at all: she pays for her bus ticket with her phone. Four people, one table and four very different childhoods.',
        'One reason why so many generations now share a table is that people live longer. According to Statistics Estonia, a baby born in Estonia in 1995 could expect to live 67.6 years. For a baby born in 2025, the figure was 79.8 years. That is more than twelve extra years within one generation. For many children, grandparents and even great-grandparents are part of everyday family life.',
        'We like to give generations names. The American Pew Research Center, for example, calls people born from 1997 onwards Generation Z and the group just before them Millennials. The labels are handy, and the media use them all the time. Yet in 2023 Pew itself announced that it would use them more carefully. Its researchers explained that one generation covers fifteen to eighteen years of births. People inside it think and live in very different ways. Young people may also differ from older people simply because they are young, not because of the year they were born.',
        'So who are we: our year of birth, our family, the place where we grew up, or the choices we make? In this module you meet people of different ages, hear how one summer day changed everyday money, and find out why young people in Estonia leave home earlier than most Europeans.',
