        'The first problem is age itself. A twenty-year-old today is different from a fifty-year-old, but that does not prove that they belong to different generations. Young adults have always been more restless, more willing to move and less interested in saving than people in their fifties. To find a real generational difference, researchers have to compare young people today with young people in the past at the same age.',
        '',
        'The second problem is history. Some events change everyone at once. When prices rise sharply or a pandemic closes schools, older and younger people both change their habits. Researchers call this a period effect. It can easily be mistaken for a difference between generations.',
        '',
        'The third problem is size. A generation usually covers fifteen to eighteen years of births. People born at the start of that period may have grown up in a completely different world from those born at the end. Inside one generation there are also rich and poor families, city and country childhoods, and many different beliefs.',
        '',
        'There is also the question of place. Most research on generations comes from the United States. A teenager in Tallinn and a teenager in Texas may both belong to Generation Z on paper, yet their schools, their politics and their family histories are very different. Labels made in one country do not always travel well.',
